# General coding rules (always follow)

## Before implementing
- Never assume unclear requirements, product behavior, API shape, or data
  format — ask or verify first. No guessing.
- Propose a short plan (files touched, structure, approach) before writing
  non-trivial code. Wait for confirmation on anything ambiguous.
- Do not take shortcuts that look correct but skip edge cases, error handling,
  or validation just to appear "done." No loopholes, no hardcoded values
  standing in for real logic unless explicitly told to mock.

## Code structure
- Imports always at the top of the file, grouped (stdlib/external/internal),
  never inline or mid-file.
- One clear responsibility per function/component. No god-functions.
- Consistent naming (no mixing camelCase/snake_case in the same language).
- No dead code, no commented-out code, no leftover debug prints/logs.
- No duplicated logic — extract shared code instead of copy-pasting.

## Comments
- Minimal comments. Only explain *why*, never *what*.
- No filler comments like "// helper function" or "// main logic here."

## Output discipline
- No placeholder/TODO code passed off as finished work.
- No unnecessary abstraction or premature optimization.
- No unrelated changes bundled into a task.
- Every function/module should be independently testable and runnable.

## Honesty
- If something can't be done as asked, or a better approach exists, say so
  directly instead of silently doing a weaker version.
- If a limitation, risk, or trade-off exists, state it plainly, don't bury it.
